    "narrative": "For this slate, **Austin Riley** emerges as our strongest play with a 63.46 efficiency score. Despite batting seventh, he benefits from a favorable matchup against Martinez, whose 4.65 xERA provides a clear runway for production. While **Isaac Paredes** (49.25) draws a difficult assignment against Wheeler, his placement in the three-hole and a 9% boost from platoon dynamics keep him viable, though he remains in the marginal tier. **Josh Naylor** (46.08) faces the stiffest test of our starters against deGrom; his upside is suppressed by the pitcher\u2019s elite skill set, forcing us to lean on his recent form to clear the minimum floor for inclusion.\n\nThe most difficult bench decision involved **Cole Young**, who landed at 31.44\u2014falling well short of the 40.0 threshold required for a start. While we prioritize speed, **Cole Young**\u2019s inability to overcome the negative park factors and deGrom\u2019s elite arm solidified the decision to keep him on the pine. We are also managing an inactive roster today, as **Corbin Carroll**, **James Wood**, and **Jose Caballero** are unavailable due to team off-days or lineup exclusion. With **Mickey Moniak**, **Trevor Story**, **Brice Turang**, **Seiya Suzuki**, **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Vinnie Pasquantino**, **Tyler Soderstrom**, **Max Muncy**, **Gabriel Moreno**, **Zach McKinstry**, **Dalton Rushing**, **Travis Bazzana**, **Griffin Conine**, and **Edwin Arroyo** also sidelined or benched, our offensive ceiling is heavily concentrated on the trio of **Austin Riley**, **Isaac Paredes**, and **Josh Naylor**. We are effectively playing a short-bench strategy today to protect our remaining game caps for higher-leverage matchups later in the week.",
